# Analysis I unchecked mathematical interfaces
# 
# This is a documentation-only ledger.  It is not a chapter, is not exported by
# litex.config, and contains no executable Litex declarations.
# 
# Snapshot update: 2026-07-28.  The Chapter 7 draft contains 32 direct `trust`
# sites and two temporary abstract graph interfaces.  Chapters 1-5, together
# with Appendix A, contain no direct `trust` statement.  Chapter 6 contains two
# non-source-deferred final-sprint blockers tracked separately.
# 
# A formalization gap is mathematics supplied or used by the source whose
# Litex proof remains unfinished.  A source-deferred proof is an unchecked
# theorem whose source explicitly says `Exercise`, `why?`, or `How?`; it is a
# visible trust boundary, not an omitted source proof.  Each item states the
# missing mathematics and the source-facing result it supports.
# 
# Chapter 7 — Infinite series (32 direct trust sites)
# 
# Formalization gaps
# 
# Items 07-01 through 07-09 — absolute convergence, harmonic divergence, and
# the alternating-series tail estimate.  Supply the quantified tail triangle
# closure, limit triangle estimate, dyadic harmonic lower bound, sign
# normalization, finite-tail successor laws, alternating-pair induction, and
# the final parity tail bound.
# 
# Items 07-10 through 07-14 — shifted absolute partial sums, Cauchy
# condensation, and p-series.  Restore the shifted-prefix bound, the strong
# condensation upper bound, reciprocal antitonicity for positive real powers,
# real-power quotient normalization, and the geometric-series convergence
# bridge used for p-series.
# 
# Items 07-15 through 07-20 — rearrangements of nonnegative series.  Prove the
# two finite-range image/preimage sum comparisons, the two order-closure facts
# for limits of increasing partial sums, and the adjacent-pair-swap function's
# positive-return well-definedness and computation rules.  The latter currently
# uses the temporary abstract graph `is_adjacent_pair_swap_value`.
# 
# Items 07-21 through 07-32 — ratio/root comparison.  Supply the
# ratio-to-product inductions, product-to-root eventual bounds, limsup/liminf
# contradictions, finite-prefix boundedness, the Chapter 6 limsup bridge,
# failure of the zero limit above ratio one, and the squeeze proof when the
# ratio tends to one.
# 
# Items 07-33 through 07-36 — absolute ratio test and the natural-root example.
# Implement the absolute-ratio sequence graph, remove its trusted unique
# selector, prove the two ratio-test conclusions, and restore the checked
# limit `n^(1/n) -> 1`.  The limit `(n+1)/n -> 1` and the final pointwise
# unfolding are now checked.  The temporary abstract graph is
# `is_absolute_ratio_sequence`.
# 
# Chapter 8 — Infinite sets (96 executable trust sites)
# 
# Formalization gaps not deferred by the source (91)
#
# The gaps cover cardinality transport, enumerations, pairing, products,
# countable unions, finite-subsums, arbitrary-set summation, Fubini, support
# transport, diagonalization, binary expansions, finite products, supremum
# sequences, and the good-chain construction.
#
# In particular, the broad trusts for the good-chain union being well-ordered
# and having no strict upper bound are not legitimate source-deferred
# boundaries: Tao supplies the main proofs and leaves only individual “why?”
# steps or Exercise 8.5.11 to the reader.  These trusts must be narrowed to
# precisely those omitted substeps or removed.
# 
# Source-deferred unchecked proofs eligible to retain with exact quotations (5)
# 
# Item 08-05 — Section 8.2;
# positive_and_negative_parts_of_conditional_series_diverge, trust at line 4628.
# Show that convergence of either sign subseries together with convergence of
# the original series would force convergence of the absolute-value series.
# This is Exercise 8.2.4 and prepares the rearrangement construction.
# 
# Item 08-06 — Section 8.2; riemann_rearrangement_theorem, trust at line 4653.
# Construct the alternating positive/negative selection permutation, prove its
# partial sums converge to the target, and retain non-absolute convergence.
# The source leaves these details to Exercise 8.2.5.
# 
# Item 08-08 — Section 8.5; finite_total_order_is_well_ordered.
# Prove by induction that each finite nonempty subset of a total order has a
# minimum.  The source assigns this fact to Exercise 8.5.8.
# 
# Item 08-09 — Section 8.5;
# good_chain_comparison_forward_from_exercise_8_5_13, trust at line 7050.
# Prove that every point of W minus Y strictly bounds Y.  This is one half of
# the source's entire Exercise 8.5.13 comparison argument.
# 
# Item 08-10 — Section 8.5;
# good_chain_comparison_reverse_from_exercise_8_5_13, trust at line 7063.
# Prove the symmetric comparison for points of Y minus W.
# 
# Chapter 9 — Continuous functions on R (6 direct trust sites)
# 
# Source-deferred unchecked proofs (6)
# 
# Item 09-01 — Section 9.4; positive_base_exponentiation_continuous.
# Prove the epsilon-delta continuity of x mapped to a^x for a fixed positive
# base using the real-exponentiation estimates of Chapter 6.  This is
# Proposition 9.4.10, whose proof is Exercise 9.4.3.
# 
# Item 09-02 — Section 9.4; positive_domain_power_function_continuous.
# Reduce continuity of x mapped to x^p on the positive real axis to
# exponentiation continuity and the real-power limit laws.  This is
# Proposition 9.4.11, whose proof is Exercise 9.4.4.
# 
# Item 09-04 — Section 9.7; continuous_image_of_interval_is_interval.
# Choose preimages of two attained values and apply the intermediate value
# theorem to every value between them.
# It is Corollary 9.7.4, whose proof is left to Exercise 9.7.1.
# 
# Item 09-05 — Section 9.8; continuous_strictly_increasing_inverse.
# Combine strict-monotonicity injectivity, intermediate-value surjectivity onto
# the image, and epsilon-delta continuity of the inverse.
# It is Proposition 9.8.3 and supports the root-construction example; the
# source leaves the combined proof to Exercise 9.8.4.
# 
# Item 09-06 — Section 9.9; uniformly_continuous_has_limit_at_adherent_point.
# Take a sequence in the domain approaching the adherent point, prove its image
# is Cauchy, and show the resulting limit is independent of the chosen sequence.
# It is Corollary 9.9.14, whose proof is Exercise 9.9.4.
# 
# Item 09-07 — Section 9.9; uniformly_continuous_maps_bounded_sets_to_bounded_sets.
# Cover a bounded set by finitely many uniformly small intervals, or derive a
# contradiction from an unbounded image sequence.
# It is Proposition 9.9.15, whose proof is Exercise 9.9.5.
# 
