# Source-deferred unchecked proofs (11)
# 
# Item 11-01 — Section 11.1; bounded_connected_subsets_are_bounded_intervals.
# Construct the endpoints of a bounded connected subset and classify the four
# open/closed endpoint cases.  It supplies Lemma 11.1.4.  The endpoint
# construction is deferred to Exercise 11.1.1.
# 
# Item 11-03 — Section 11.4; riemann_integral_extension_by_zero.
# Compare upper and lower approximations after extending an integrable function
# by zero from I to a larger bounded interval J.  The two conclusions are
# integrability of the extension and equality of the two integrals.  They are
# Theorem 11.4.1(g), left to Exercise 11.4.1.
# 
# Item 11-04 — Section 11.4; riemann_integral_additive_over_two_piece_partition.
# Compare Darboux sums over two interval pieces and glue their restrictions
# back into the whole interval.  The three conclusions are integrability on
# each piece and additivity of their integrals.  They are Theorem 11.4.1(h),
# left to Exercise 11.4.1.
# 
# Item 11-05 — Section 11.5; uniformly_continuous_on_bounded_interval_has_small_oscillation_partitions.
# Construct a sufficiently fine finite partition in all endpoint conventions
# and prove the oscillation estimate on each piece.  It supplies the partition
# step in Theorem 11.5.1; the source marks the construction as "How?".
# 
# Item 11-08 — Section 11.5; riemann_integrable_from_partition_piece_integrability.
# Induct over a finite interval partition and compare the restricted piece
# integrals with the whole-domain Darboux sums.  It supplies Proposition 11.5.6
# and is left to Exercise 11.5.1.
# 
# Item 11-10 — Section 11.6; bounded_monotone_bounded_interval_riemann_integrable.
# Trim an arbitrary bounded interval to closed subintervals and control its
# endpoint tails using boundedness.  It supplies Corollary 11.6.3, whose proof
# is Exercise 11.6.1.
# 
# Item 11-11 — Section 11.6; integral_test_series_implies_finite_integral_supremum.
# Compare finite sums with integrals for a decreasing non-negative function and
# bound the integral supremum by convergent partial sums.  This is one direction
# of Proposition 11.6.4, left to Exercise 11.6.3.
# 
# Item 11-12 — Section 11.6; integral_test_finite_integral_supremum_implies_series.
# Use the reverse rectangle comparison to bound partial sums and obtain a real
# series sum.  It is the other direction of Proposition 11.6.4, left to
# Exercise 11.6.3.
# 
# Item 11-13 — Section 11.8; alpha_interval_length_adds_across_bounded_difference.
# Classify endpoint forms and telescope the alpha-values when a bounded
# subinterval is removed.  It supplies finite additivity of alpha-length in
# Lemma 11.8.4 and is left to Exercise 11.8.1.
# 
# Item 11-20 — Section 11.10; change_of_variables_piecewise_constant, preimage partition.
# Construct the preimage partition under the increasing change of variables
# and prove that every preimage piece is an interval.  It supplies the
# piecewise-constant structural step in Lemma 11.10.5; the source leaves the
# marked "why?" gaps to Exercise 11.10.2.
# 
# Item 11-21 — Section 11.10; change_of_variables_piecewise_constant, integral identity.
# Prove that phi maps each preimage piece to a piece of the same length and sum
# the resulting finite identities.  The conclusions are Stieltjes
# integrability of the composition and equality with the original Riemann
# integral in Lemma 11.10.5; they are left to Exercise 11.10.2.
